      "role": "user",
      "content": "Write the test plan for assignment `button_toggle_opus_a`.\n\n<handout>\n# Lab 4: Debounced button toggle (mock handout)\n\n> Mock handout written for testing `grader make-plan`. It is not an official course\n> document.\n\n## Goal\n\nUse a push button to toggle an LED, and report each change over a serial port.\n\n## Hardware\n\n* A push button between pin **D2** and GND. There is no external resistor, so use the\n  internal pull-up (`INPUT_PULLUP`). The button reads LOW while pressed.\n* The on-board LED on pin **D13**.\n* **Serial1** (pins D0/D1), not the USB `Serial` port, at **9600 baud**.\n\n## Requirements\n\n1. The LED is **off** after reset.\n2. Each press of the button toggles the LED: the first press turns it on, the next one\n   turns it off, and so on.\n3. The LED must react **quickly**: a user should not notice any delay.\n4. Real buttons bounce. One press must toggle the LED exactly **once**, no matter how\n   much the contacts bounce.\n5. On every toggle, print `ON` or `OFF` (the new state) on its own line on Serial1.\n6. Implement debouncing in software, without blocking `delay()` calls.\n7. Comment your code.\n\n## Submission\n\nSubmit your sketch folder.\n</handout>\n\n<pin_map>\n# Student board (Arduino Nano 33 BLE Sense) pin name -> measuring board (Nucleo-L476RG) pin.\n# Capabilities are from the MEASURING board's point of view (uart_rx = STM32 receives).\n#\n# !!! Every stm32 value is a PLACEHOLDER until verified in STM32CubeMX against the wiring. !!!\npins:\n  D5:  { stm32: PA0,  caps: [gpio, timer_capture] }   # Nucleo A0, TIM2_CH1 (FREQ)\n  D2:  { stm32: PA8,  caps: [gpio, drive] }           # Nucleo D7, via 1 kOhm; STM32 drives the student's button input\n  D13: { stm32: PB3,  caps: [gpio, timer_capture] }   # Nucleo D3, TIM2_CH2; Nano on-board LED\n  D1:  { stm32: PA10, caps: [uart_rx] }               # Nucleo D2, Nano Serial1 TX -> STM32 USART1 RX\n  D0:  { stm32: PA9,  caps: [uart_tx] }               # Nucleo D8, via 1 kOhm; Nano Serial1 RX <- STM32 USART1 TX\n  A0:  { stm32: TBD,  caps: [analog_in, dac] }        # placeholder; L476 DAC1_OUT1 is PA4\n  A4:  { stm32: TBD,  caps: [i2c_sda] }               # placeholder, STM32 acts as I2C target\n  A5:  { stm32: TBD,  caps: [i2c_scl] }               # placeholder\n  # Rig only, never used by a plan: open-drain line from the STM32 to the Nano's RST pin.\n  RST: { stm32: PA1,  caps: [reset] }                 # Nucleo A1 -> Nano RST (open-drain, pulled low to reset)\n</pin_map>\n\n<primitives>\nEach measurement primitive and the result fields it returns (type, unit, and when it is null):\n- freq_duty:\n    - frequency_hz: number, Hz; null if fewer than 2 rising edges (no signal)\n    - duty_pct: number, % of the period spent high (0-100); null if no signal\n    - edge_count: integer, rising edges in the window\n- edge_delay:\n    - delay_ms: number, ms from the first from_edge on from_pin to the first to_edge on to_pin; null if to_pin never changed within timeout_ms\n    - to_edge_count: integer, to_edge edges on to_pin from the trigger until timeout_ms\n- logic_trace:\n    - edge_count: integer, edges in either direction during the window\n    - final_level: integer, 0 (low) or 1 (high) at the end of the window\n    - edges_ms: list of numbers, ms of each edge from the start of the window\n- uart_read:\n    - text: string, the bytes received (undecodable bytes become U+FFFD)\n    - byte_count: integer, bytes received\n    - framing_errors: integer, bytes received with framing errors (e.g. wrong baud)\n- i2c_log:\n    - read_count: integer, read transactions\n    - write_count: integer, write transactions\n    - transactions: list of strings such as \"W:0x68:0x3B\" or \"R:0x68:0x3B:6\"\n- analog_read:\n    - mean_mv: number, mV\n    - min_mv: number, mV\n    - max_mv: number, mV\n</primitives>"
    },
